//! # Tests, documentation examples, and benchmarks
//!
//! `hew test path` discovers `#[test]` functions recursively. Use `--list`
//! when discovery is surprising and `--filter text` to run matching names.
//! A test is an ordinary function with no parameters; assertions and panics
//! fail it.
//!
//! ```hew
//! fn add(left: i64, right: i64) -> i64 { left + right }
//!
//! #[test]
//! fn adds_values() {
//!     assert(add(20, 22) == 42);
//! }
//!
//! #[test]
//! #[should_panic("expected failure")]
//! fn checks_panics() {
//!     panic("expected failure");
//! }
//! ```
//!
//! `#[ignore("reason")]` keeps a test discoverable but skips it by default.
//! `#[timeout(2s)]` applies a positive wall-clock guard. `#[serial]` prevents
//! overlap with other serial tests, and `#[real_time]` chooses the threaded
//! scheduler and host clock instead of deterministic scheduling. These are
//! modifiers on the same `#[test]` function, not standalone test markers.
//!
//! Documentation fences run with `hew test --doc`. Mark a fence `no_run` when
//! it must compile but depends on a host service, or `ignore` only when it
//! intentionally demonstrates rejected or incomplete source. A trailing
//! `// Output:` block makes printed output part of the check.
//!
//! `#[bench]` marks a benchmark taking one
//! [std.bench.Bencher](std.bench.html); run it with `hew bench`. Use tests for
//! correctness and benchmarks for optimized host-clock measurement.
